# Lanthanide-organic frameworks based on terphenyl-tetracarboxylate ligands: syntheses, structures, optical properties and selective sensing of nitro explosives

1Key Laboratory for Organic Electronics and Information Displays, Institute of Advanced Materials, Nanjing University of Posts & Telecommunications, Nanjing 210023, China 2Key Laboratory of Flexible Electronics, Institute of Advanced Materials, Jiangsu National Synergetic Innovation Center for Advanced Materials, Nanjing Tech University, Nanjing 211816, China 3School of College of Opto-Electronic Engineering, Nanjing University of Posts and Telecommunications, Nanjing 210023, China
Three isostructural three-dimensional (3D) lanthanide-based metal-organic frameworks $\mathrm{\small{[Ln_{2}L(H_{2}L)(N M P)_{2}]}\cdot H_{2}O}$ $\mathrm{Ln}{=}\mathrm{Sm}$ (1), Eu (2), Gd (3); $_{\mathrm{H_{4}L=1},1^{\prime}:4^{\prime},1"}$ -terphenyl- $^{2^{\prime},4,4^{\prime\prime},5^{\prime}}$ -tetracarboxylic acid; ${\mathrm{NMP}}{=}N $ -methyl-2-pyrrolidone) have been synthesized and structurally characterized. In 1–3, two $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions are doubly-bridged by two oxygen atoms of two carboxylate groups to form the dinuclear $\mathrm{Ln}_{2}(\mathrm{Ocoo^{-}})_{2}$ unit. Each $\mathrm{Ln}_{2}(\mathrm{Ocoo^{-}})_{2}$ unit links with four $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ ligands and four $\mathrm{L}^{4-}$ ligands to lead to the 3D framework, which can be rationalized as a new trinodal 4,4,8-connected $(4^{4}.6^{2})(4^{5}.6)(4^{12}.6^{16})$ topological network by considering the dinuclear $\mathrm{Ln}_{2}(\mathrm{Ocoo^{-}})_{2}$ units as 8-connected nodes and $\mathrm{L^{4-}/H_{2}L^{2-}}$ ligands as planar 4-connected nodes, respectively. 1 and 3 exhibit blue emission originated from the ligand with the emission maximum at $384~\mathrm{nm}$ , while 2 shows intense characteristic red emission of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions and weak ligand-centered emission. Moreover, 2 has fluorescent quenching response towards the aromatic nitro compounds, especially for the 3,4-dinitrotoluene (3,4-DNT) with the linear Stern-Volmer relationship in the concentration range of $0{\mathrm{-}}1{\mathrm{~mM}}$ and the quenching constant $(K_{\mathrm{sv}})$ of $2.084\times10^{3}\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ .
lanthanide, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), sensor, nitro explosives
Citation: Zhou X, Zhu Y, Li L, Yang T, Wang J, Huang W. Lanthanide-organic frameworks based on terphenyl-tetracarboxylate ligands: syntheses, structures, optical properties and selective sensing of nitro explosives. Sci China Chem, 2017, 60: doi: 10.1007/s11426-016-9006-x
## 1  Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a new class of crystalline materials, which are comprised of metal ions and bridging organic ligands, have attracted great attention owing to their extensive applications in light emitting [1], gas storage and separation [2,3], heterogeneous catalysis [4], chemical sensing [5], magnetic properties [2,6] and drug delivery [7]. Recently, lanthanide-based metal-organic frameworks (LnOFs) have received growing attention, because of their diversified topological structures [8,9] and the unique optical properties [10], such as high color purity, visible light emission, large Stokes shift, and relatively long luminescence lifetimes, which originally result from f-f transition [11,12]. So far, a variety of aromatic-carboxylate or pyridine-carboxylate ligands with high sensitized effect to lanthanide ions have been reported in previous work [13–15].
Nitro explosives not only are the notorious environmental pollutants [16], but also have become a major threat to mankind health and national homeland security, which prompt the scientific community to develop new detection methods. For decades, numerous new ways to detect nitro explosives, such as gas chromatogramphy coupled with mass spectrometry, Raman spectroscopy, neutron activation analysis, X-ray imaging and ion mobility spectroscopy (IMS) have been developed [17–21]. However, low portability, complex pretreatments and expensive instruments have greatly limited their widespread use. Since LnOFs as luminescence probes have unique advantages, such as nondestructive detection, high sensitivity, fast response time and real-time monitoring [21], many LnOFs have been employed for detection of nitro explosives. Qin et al. [22] reported two luminescent LnOFs, the calculated quenching constant $(K_{\mathrm{sv}})$ values for nitrobenzene (NB), 2-nitrotoluene (2-NT), and 2,4,6-trinitrophenol (TNP) were $6.17\times10^{-2}$ , $2.87\times10^{-2}$ , and $6.76{\times}10^{-2}\ \mathrm{ppm}^{-1}$ for Eu-MOF, and $7.54\times10^{-2}$ , $7.46\times10^{-2}$ , and $3.25{\times}10^{-2}\mathrm{ppm}^{-1}$ for Tb-MOF, respectively. Wang et al. [23] reported a Tb-MOF, its emission intensity displaying an ideal linear correlation with the concentration of NB (from 50 ppm to $1000\mathrm{ppm})$ .
Based on the above considerations, we are devoted to exploring LnOF-based sensing systems towards nitro explosives. In this work, we prepared three new LnOFs $(\mathrm{[Ln_{2}L(H_{2}L)(N M P)_{2}]{\cdot}H_{2}O}$ , where $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Ln}}={\mathrm{Sm}}$ (1), Eu (2), Gd (3); $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}{=}1,1{'}{:}4{'},1{"}$ -terphenyl- $^{2^{\prime},4,4^{\prime\prime},5^{\prime}}$ -tetracarboxylic acid; ${\mathrm{NMP}}=N.$ -methyl-2-pyrrolidone, by solvothermal synthesis. Next, we will give a detailed introduction of their syntheses, structures, luminescence properties and the sensing of nitro explosives.
## 2  Experimental
## 2.1  Materials and measurement
$\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}$ was purchased from the Jinan Henghua Sci & Tec Co., Ltd. (China). The other chemicals employed in the present work were of analytical grade as obtained from commercial sources and used without further purification.
Elemental analyses for C, H and $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ were performed on a Perkin-Elmer 240C analyzer (USA). Infrared spectra were recorded with KBr pellets in the $400{\-}4000\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ region on a PerkinElmer FT-IR Spectrometer (USA). Powder X-ray diffraction investigation on polycrystalline samples were carried out with a Bruker D8 advanced diffractometer (Germany) equipped with a diffracted-beamed monochromator set for $\mathrm{{Cu-Ka}}$ $\begin{array}{r}{(\lambda{=}1.5418\mathrm{~}\mathrm{\AA}}\\ {(\lambda{=}1.5418\mathrm{~}\mathrm{\AA})}\end{array}$ ) radiation. The data were collected at room temperature in the $2\theta$ range from $5^{\circ}$ to $50^{\circ}$ at an angular rate of $0.1\mathrm{~s~}\mathrm{step}^{-1}$ , with a scan step width of $0.02^{\circ}$ . The thermogravimetric analyses (TGA) were performed in a SHIMADZU DTG-60 simultaneous DTA-TG apparatus instrument (Japan), under dynamic ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere $(20\ \mathrm{mL\min^{-1}})$ ) and heating rate of $10\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ from room temperature to $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## 2.2 Photoluminescence measurements
The photoluminescence properties of 1–3 were investigated in N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) solvent as LnOF-solvent emulsions at room temperature. The LnOF-solvent emulsions were prepared by introducing $3~\mathrm{mg}$ of powder into $3~\mathrm{mL}$ solvent and ultrasonic agitation for $15\ \mathrm{min}$ before testing. Photoluminescent spectra were measured using a PerkinElmer LS55 Fluorescence Spectrometer (USA). Time-resolved photoluminescence measurements were performed using a Life-Spec-ps photoluminescence lifetime analytical spectrometer (Edinburgh Instruments, UK).
2.3  Synthesis of $[\mathbf{L}\mathbf{n}_{2}\mathbf{L}(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{L})(\mathbf{N}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{P})_{2}]{\cdot}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\ 1{-}3$
A mixture of $_\mathrm{H_{4}L}$ ( $10.2~\mathrm{mg},~0.025~\mathrm{mmol})$ , $\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ 1 $\mathrm{Ln}{=}\mathrm{Sm}$ , Eu, Gd) $(0.025\mathrm{mmol})$ , NMP $3~\mathrm{mL}$ ), $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ ( $3~\mathrm{mL}$ and concentrated hydrochloric acid $(50~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ was sealed in a $15~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave and heated at $160^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $3\mathrm{d}$ . Then, the reaction mixture was slowly cooled to room temperature. Colorless block crystals of 1–3 were collected by filtration and dried in air at ambient temperature. Elemental analysis calcd. for $\mathrm{C_{54}H_{42}N_{2}O_{19}S m_{2}\uparrow^{0}}$ : C, 49.00; H, 3.20; N, 2.12. Found: C, 48.94; H, 3.23; N, 2.15. Elemental analysis calcd. for $\mathrm{C_{54}H_{42}E u_{2}N_{2}O_{19}\ (\%)}$ : C, 48.88; H, 3.19; N, 2.11. Found: C, 48.80; H, 3.10; N, 2.05. Elemental analysis calcd. for $\mathrm{C_{54}H_{42}G d_{2}N_{2}O_{19}}$ $(\%)$ : C, 48.50; H, 3.17; N, 2.09. Found: C, 48.64; H, 3.23; N, 2.15. IR (KBr pellet, $\mathsf{c m}^{-1},$ for 1: 3455 s, 1928 w, 1622 s, 1580 s, 1411 s, $1349\mathrm{m}$ , $1253\mathrm{m}$ , 1012 w, 864 s, 769 s, 716 s, 579 m. IR (KBr pellet, $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ ) for 2: 3475 s, 1916 w, 1653 s, 1579 s, 1422 s, $1348\mathrm{m}$ , 1264 m, 1011 w, 863 s, 769 s, 705 s, 579 m. IR (KBr pellet, $\mathsf{c m}^{-1},$ for 3: 3435 s, 1929 w, 1644 s, 1560 s, 1412 s, $1350\mathrm{m}$ , 1265 m, 1013 w, 865 s, 770 s, 717 s, $580\mathrm{m}$ .
## 2.4  X-ray crystallography
X-ray diffraction data of the single crystals of 1–3 were collected on a Bruker Smart Apex CCD area detector diffractometer (Germany) using graphite-monochromated $_{\mathrm{Mo-Ka}}$ radiation $(\lambda{=}0.71073\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ). Cell parameters were retrieved using SMART software and refined using SAINT [24] on all observed reflections. Data were collected using a narrow-frame method with scan widths of $0.30^{\circ}$ in $\omega$ . The highly redundant data sets were reduced using SAINT and corrected for Lorentz and polarization effects. Absorption corrections were applied using SADABS [25] supplied by Bruker. Structures were solved by direct methods using the program SHELX-97 [26] program package. The positions of the metal atoms and their first coordination spheres were located from direct-method E maps; other non-hydrogen atoms were found using alternating difference Fourier syntheses and least-squared refinement cycles and, during the final cycles, were refined anisotropically. H8A, H10A and H10B were found on the difference Fourier map and subsequently refined with an isotropic displacement parameter. The other hydrogen atoms were placed in calculated positions and refined as riding atoms with a uniform value of $U_{\mathrm{iso}}$ . Crystallographic details for 1–3 have been summarized in Table 1. The selected bond lengths and bond angles are listed in Table S1 (Supporting Information online). CCDC reference numbers 1515574 for 1, 1515575 for 2, and 1515576 for 3.
## 3  Results and discussion
## 3.1  Synthesis
Compounds 1–3 were prepared by self-assemblies of $_\mathrm{H_{4}L}$ and lanthanide salts under solvothermal conditions. In structures, there are partly deprotonated ligands, in agreement with the IR data (Figure S1, Supporting Information online), in which the strong absorption peaks around $1700\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for the –COOH group are observed.
## 3.2  Structures of 1–3
Single-crystal analyses reveal that 1, 2 and 3 are isostructural. Hence, only the structure of 2 is selected as a representative to be described here in detail. In the asymmetric unit of 2, there are one ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ion, half a $\mathrm{L}^{4-}$ ligand, half a $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ ligand, one coordinated NMP molecule (except O9, the NMP molecule is disordered over two sites with about $49\%:51\%$ split occupancies) and half a free $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ molecule (Figure 1(a)). The ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ion is coordinated by nine oxygen atoms, four (O1, O2, O3 and O4) of which are from two different $\mathrm{L}^{4-}$ ligands, four (O5, O5 A, O6 and O7B) of which are from three different $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ ligands and O9 from the coordinated NMP molecule, to form the tricapped trigonal prismatic coordination polyhedron. The $_\mathrm{Ln-O}$ bond distances range from 2.362(2) Å to 2.531(2) Å for 1, from 2.356(2) $\textup{\AA}$ to 2.528(2) Å for 2, from 2.330(2) Å to 2.506(2) Å for 3, which are all comparable to those found in other ${\mathrm{Ln}}^{3+}$ complexes. The $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ ligand bridges six ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions with each of two oxygen atoms from two carboxylate groups in the terminal phenyl rings linking one ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ion, and each of two carboxylate groups in the central phenyl ring linking two ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions through the chelating and monodentate coordination modes. The $\mathrm{L}^{4-}$ ligand bridges four ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions through each carboxylate group chelating to a ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ion (Figure 1(b)). Two oxygen atoms in the central phenyl rings from two $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ ligands doubly-bridge two ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions to form the dinuclear $\mathrm{Eu_{2}(O_{C O O}-)_{2}}$ unit. Each $\mathrm{Eu}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{COO}^{-}})_{2}$ unit links with four $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ ligands and each $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ ligand links with four $\mathrm{Eu}_{2}(\mathrm{Ocoo}^{-})_{2}$ units to form the parallel arranged two-dimensional nets lying on the $a b$ crystal plane. Each $\mathrm{Eu}_{2}(\mathrm{Ocoo}^{-})_{2}$ unit in the 2D net further links with four $\mathrm{L}^{4-}$ ligands, of which, two on the side of the 2D net, two on the other side. Each $\mathrm{L}^{4-}$ ligand links with four $\mathrm{Eu_{2}(O_{C O O}-)_{2}}$ units, of which, two from a 2D net, two from another neighboring 2D net. Consequently, the 2D nets are connected with each other to lead to the three-dimensional framework (Figure 1(c)). The 3D framework can be rationalized as a trinodal 4,4,8-connected $(4^{4}.6^{2})(4^{5}.6)(4^{12}.6^{16})$ topological network (the long Schläfli symbol is $(4.4.4_{2}.4_{2}.6_{4}.6_{4})(4.4.4.4.4_{2}.6_{2})\t$ (4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4. $4.4.4.4.4.6.6.6.6.6_{2}.6_{2}.6_{2}.6_{2}.6_{2}.6_{2}.6_{2}.6_{2}.6_{2}.6_{2}.6_{2}.6_{2}.6_{2}.6_{6})$ ) by considering the dinuclear $\mathrm{Eu}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{COO}^{-}})_{2}$ units as 8-connected nodes and $\mathrm{L^{4-}/H_{2}L^{2-}}$ ligands as planar 4-connected nodes, respectively (Figure 1(d)), which represents a new topology analyzed by the TOPOS 4.0 program [27].
## 3.3  PXRD and thermal stabilities of 1–3
Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) of 1–3 confirmed the phase purity of their bulk crystalline materials. As shown in Figure S2, the peaks on the PXRD of as-synthesized samples of 1–3 are coincident with those on their simulated patterns. The differences in reflection intensity between the simulated and experimental patterns are due to the variation in crystal orientation of the powder sample. The framework stabilities of
2 in various solvents have also been checked by collecting PXRD patterns after the samples dipped into the solvents for $24\mathrm{h}$ . And the result shows the sample is stable in these solvents.
To examine the thermal stability of 1–3, the thermogravimetric analyses (TGA) of 1–3 were conducted (Figure S3). The weight losses of $8.3\%$ (calc. $8.8\%$ ) for 1 in the range from room temperature to $363~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $8.3\%$ (calc. $8.8\%$ ) for 2 in the range from room temperature to $356~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $8.7\%$ (calc. $8.8\%$ ) for 3 in the range from room temperature to $343~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , correspond to the release of one free water molecule and one coordinated NMP.
## 3.4  Photoluminescent properties
1 and 3 exhibit a strong fluorescent emission band with the emission maximum at $384~\mathrm{nm}$ when excited at $335~\mathrm{nm}$ at room temperature (Figure 2 and Figure S4), blue-shifted by about $29\mathrm{nm}$ compared to that of the free $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}$ ligand [28]. 2 exhibits the strong characteristic red emission of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ion with emission peaks at 582, 593, 617, 648 and $700\mathrm{nm}$ arising from the ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}{\rightarrow}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{J}$ $\scriptstyle(J=0-4)$ transitions upon excitation at $340~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 2 and Figure S4). The weak ligand-centered emission with the maximum at $424~\mathrm{nm}$ is also observed in the fluorescent spectrum of 2, red-shifted by about $11\ \mathrm{nm}$ compared to that of the free $_\mathrm{H_{4}L}$ ligand. It indicates that ligand adsorbs and transfers the energy to ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ion by antenna effect.
## 3.5  Detection for nitro compounds
To study the luminescent responses to small molecule solvents, 2 was dispersed $(3~\mathrm{mg}/3~\mathrm{mL}$ ) in ethanol (EtOH), dichloromethane (DCM), methanol (MeOH), acetone $\mathrm{(Me_{2}C O)}$ , dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO), $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , tetrahydrofuran (THF), DMF and nitrobenzene (NB), respectively. The result shows that the luminescence was completely quenched when 2 was dispersed in NB (Figure 3 and Figure S5), suggesting that 2 maybe a potential luminescent sensor for nitro compounds. So, the luminescent responses of 2 to $0.1\mathrm{\mM}$ nitro compounds such as 3,4-dinitrotoluene (3,4-DNT), 2,4-dinitrotoluene (2,4-DNT), 4-nitrotoluene (4-NT), 2-NT, 1,3-dinitrobenzene (1,3-DNB), TNP, NB, 2,3-dimethyl-2,3-dinitrobutane (DMNB) were measured by dispersing 2 (3 mg) in DMF $(3\mathrm{mL})$ ). It exhibited that all eight nitro compounds can quench the photoluminescent intensity of 2 in varying degree. By monitoring emission peak at 617 nm, the quenching percentages $(Q P{=}(I_{0}{-}I)/I_{0}{\times}100\%,$ , where $I_{0}$ and $I$ are luminescence intensities of 2 before and after exposure to the nitro compounds, respectively) for various nitro compounds were obtained (Figure 4(a)). The most effective quencher is 3,4-DNT with $\mathcal{Q}P$ of $13.96\%$ , and the less effective one is DMNB. According to our previous work, the luminescent quenching may be due to the absorption of nitro explosives for the excited light source energy [15].
To further understand the quenching phenomenon of nitro compounds for 2, concentration-dependent luminescence measurement for 3,4-DNT was carried out. It was found that the luminescent intensities of the suspension of 2 in DMF decreased with the addition of 3,4-DNT, and the photoluminescence almost disappeared when the concentration was up to $51~\mathrm{mM}$ (Figure 4(b)). Moreover, the Stern-Volmer (SV) equation, $I_{0}/I{=}K_{\mathrm{sv}}[\mathrm{Q}]{+}1$ (where $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ is the quenching constant, and [Q] is the concentration of the analyte [29]) was used for the analysis  of concentration-dependent  luminescence data.
As shown in Figure 4(c), the $I_{0}/I$ exhibits a good linear dependence relation ( $R^{2}{=}0.9972$ ) with the concentrations of 3,4- DNT in the range of $0{\mathrm{-}}1{\mathrm{~mM}}$ , with slope $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ of SV curve of $2.084\times10^{3}\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ . The linear Stern-Volmer relationship indicates that either a static or dynamic quenching process is dominant. The fluorescent decay curves of 2 were measured and remained the same in different concentrations of 3,4-DNT in DMF (Figure S6), suggesting that there are no interactions between analytes and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ and changes in emission intensity are related to the interactions between the analytes and ligands. The repeatability of the luminescent sensor was demonstrated by the reversible luminescence response by successive addition and removal of 3,4-DNT for several cycles (Figure S7).
## 4  Conclusions
In summary, three isostructural luminescent LnOFs $(\mathrm{Sm}\left(\mathbf{1}\right)$ , Eu (2) and Gd (3)) based on $1,1^{\prime}{:}4^{\prime},1^{\prime\prime}$ -terphenyl- $^{2^{\prime},4,4^{\prime\prime},5^{\prime}.}$ - tetracarboxylic acid ligands have been synthesized. Their 3D frameworks represent a new trinodal 4,4,8-connected topology network with the Schläfli symbol of $(4^{4}.6^{2})(4^{5}.6)(4^{12}.6^{16})$ . Luminescence studies show that 1 and 3 exhibit blue emission arising from the organic ligand, 2 possesses the strong characteristic emissions of the ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions and weak ligand-centered emission. Especially, 2 has luminescent quenching responses towards nitro compounds. For 3,4-DNT, the linear Stern-Volmer relationship with $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ of $2.084\times10^{3}\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ was observed.
